Beckn Implementation Guide – Soil Testing Use Case

Introduction:
This document provides the implementation guideline for integrating soil testing purchase flow over the Beckn-enabled UKI Network. It defines the messaging flows, payload specifications, taxonomy usage, and other supporting configurations to enable seamless interoperability between Beckn Network Participants (BAPs and BPPs) for soil testing use cases. It assumes the reader has a good overview of the Beckn network, its APIs, the overall structure of the schema etc.

Structure of the document:
Outcome Visualization - This is a pictorial or descriptive representation of the different use cases that are supported by the network.
Flow diagrams - This section provides a pictorial representation of the message flows that happen during the use case.
API Calls and Schema - This section provides details on the API calls and the schema of the message that is sent in the form of sample schemas.
Taxonomy and layer 2 configuration - This section provides details on the taxonomy, enumerations and any rules defined for either the use case or by the network.
Notes on writing/integrating with your own software - This section describes ways in which you can integrate (Becknify) your new or existing software
Links to downloadable resources - This section contains the downloadable files referenced in this document.
Sandbox Details - Sandbox links to BAP, Registry/Gateway and BPP.

Outcome Visualization:
Smita, a farmer from Nashik, wants to test the quality of her soil to plan agri inputs and choose the right crop for better yield.
She uses a Beckn-enabled app integrated with the UKI Network to search for soil testing centres and receive detailed soil analysis.
She searches for soil testing centres via the BAP app, supporting multilingual input.
The app displays a list of soil testing service providers (BPPs) and allows Smita to filter results by:
Rating
Farm/sample collection location
Cost
Preferred date and time
Collection type:
Pick-up from farm (by provider)
Delivery to testing centre (by farmer)
Required test types:
Soil Tests:
NPK (Primary Nutrients)
Ca, Mg, S (Secondary Nutrients)
EC, pH, OC (Electrical Conductivity, pH, Organic Carbon)
Zn, B, Cu, Fe, Mo, Mn (Micronutrients)
Others: Soil Texture, Soil Moisture, Contaminants, CEC
All parameters
Water Test
Chemical Test
Smita selects Krishi Kendra Soil Services as the provider.
The provider responds with:
Available time slots
Service quote (based on collection type)
Pre-requisites for soil sample collection (format: video/image/audio/PDF)
Terms and conditions (cancellations, returns/refunds)
Provider may optionally request crop-related details:
Current Crop Info: Category, Type, Crop, Variety
Previous Crop Info: Same as above
Previous Yield (optional)
Smita accepts the service terms, selects Cash on Delivery as payment mode, and confirms the order.
Provider confirms booking, shares Order ID, and details of agent and scheduled time.
Fulfilment Scenarios:
Farm Collection:
Smita receives updates on the collection day.
Service provider visits the farm, collects the sample.
Smita pays in cash.
Collection at Testing Centre:
Smita collects and prepares soil as instructed.
She delivers the sample to the centre and pays in cash.
Smita receives:
Ongoing status updates on testing.
A final report (PDF) and intervention recommendations (via video, PDF, or image).
Post-Fulfilment:
Smita rates the service provider on:
Product/service quality
Provider behavior (punctuality, politeness)
Support received
The provider may request additional feedback.
Smita can contact support services if needed.

Flow Diagrams
General Beckn message flow and error handling

This section is relevant to all the message flows illustrated below and discussed further in the document.

Beckn is a synchronous protocol at its core.

When a network participant(NP1) sends a message to another participant(NP2), the other participant(NP2) immediately returns back an ACK/NACK(Acknowledgement or Negative Acknowledgement in case of error - usually with wrongly formed messages).
An ACK is an indicator that the receiving participant(NP2) will process this message and dispatch an on_xxxxxx message to original NP (NP1)
Subsequently after processing the message NP2 sends back the real response in the corresponding on_xxxxxx message, to which again the first participant(NP1).
This message can contain a message field (for success) or error field (for failure)
NP1 when it receives the on_xxxxxx message, sends back an ACK/NACK (Here in both the cases NP1 will not send any subsequent message).
In the Use case diagrams, this ACK/NACK is not illustrated explicitly to keep the diagrams crisp.
However when writing software we should be prepared to receive these NACK messages as well as error field in the on_xxxxxx messages
While this discussion is from a Beckn perspective, Adapters can provide synchronous modes. For example, the Protocol Server which is the reference implementation of the Beckn Adapter provides a synchronous mode by default. So if your software calls the support endpoint on the BAP Protocol Server, the Protocol Server waits till it gets the on_support and returns back that as the response.



Structure of a message with a NACK

```
{
    "message": {
        "ack": {
            "status": "NACK"
        }
    },
    "error": {
        "code": 400,
        "message": "OpenApiValidator Error at BAP-CLIENT",
    }
}
```

Structure of a on_select message with an error
```
{
    "context": {
        "action": "on_select",
        "version": "1.1.0",
        ...
    },
    "error": {
        "code": 30001,
        "message": "Requested provider is not in the database"
    }
}
```

API Calls and Schema
1. search 
Purpose: The search API is invoked by Smita’s BAP to express her intent to discover soil testing service providers on the UKI network. She specifies her requirements, such as the types of tests needed (e.g., NPK, pH, Organic Carbon), and her preferred fulfillment type (farm pickup). The search message encapsulates this intent in a structured way so that BPPs offering matching services can respond with appropriate catalogs.
Request Payload:
{
  "context": {
    "domain": "agri-services:uki",
    "action": "search",
    "version": "1.1.0",
    "bap_id": "soiltest.bap.uki.in",
    "bap_uri": "https://soiltest.bap.uki.in",
    "transaction_id": "7e92a1d4-3fa2-48e7-944d-01a6c02fa98b",
    "message_id": "ffccbc41-782b-4cc9-9822-3ca2d3f87df0",
    "ttl": "PT20S",
    "timestamp": "2025-04-29T09:00:00Z",
    "location": {
      "city": {
        "code": "std:95253",
        "name": "Nashik"
      },
      "country": {
        "code": "IND",
        "name": "India"
      }
    }
  },
  "message": {
    "intent": {
      "item": {
        "descriptor": {
          "name": "Soil Testing Service"
        },
        "tags": [
          {
            "descriptor": {
              "name": "test-types"
            },
            "list": [
              {
                "value": "NPK"
              },
              {
                "value": "pH"
              },
              {
                "value": "OC"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "fulfillment": {
        "type": "Sample-PickUp",
        "stops": [
          {
            "type": "start",
            "location": {
              "gps": "19.9975,73.7898"
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
2. on_search 
Purpose: The on_search API is the callback message sent by the BPP to the BAP containing a catalog of available soil testing services matching the search criteria. Each catalog entry includes provider information, offered services (items), fulfillment options (farm pickup or lab drop-off), and indicative pricing. Smita can then browse these catalog entries on the BAP UI to make an informed selection. Note that multiple providers and multiple service items can be returned in a single catalog.
Response Payload:
{
  "context": {
    "domain": "agri.uki",
    "action": "on_search",
    "version": "1.1.0",
    "location": {
      "city": {
        "code": "std:95253",
        "name": "Nashik"
